```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <unistd.h>

int main() {
    pid_t pid = fork();
    if (pid < 0) {
        perror("fork failed");
    } else if (pid == 0) {
        printf("child: my pid=%d, parent's pid=%d\n", getpid(), getppid());
    } else {
        printf("parent: my pid=%d, child's pid=%d\n", getpid(), pid);
    }
    return 0;
}
```

This prints **two lines**, one from each process, in an order that isn't guaranteed — the OS scheduler decides which of parent or child runs first. Both branches of the `if` genuinely execute, just in different processes; a beginner mistake is expecting only one branch to run, as you would with an ordinary `if`/`else`.

A negative return means `fork()` failed (usually a resource limit like max processes per user) and no child was created at all.
